    </div>

    <div class="card panel">
      <h3 class="gradient-text mun-heading">Model United Nations</h3>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:900px; text-align: justify;">
        Model United Nations (MUN) is an academic simulation where students represent countries,
        debate global problems, and write diplomatic documents. It combines public speaking,
        research, negotiation, teamwork, and formal writing in one activity.
      </p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>What you do in MUN</h4>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Represent a country or a delegation.</li>
            <li>Research a topic from that country's perspective.</li>
            <li>Write a position paper before the conference.</li>
            <li>Debate, negotiate, and form alliances during committee.</li>
            <li>Write and support resolutions that propose solutions.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Why it helps self-development</h4>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Improves confidence in public speaking.</li>
            <li>Builds research and critical thinking skills.</li>
            <li>Teaches diplomatic language and formal communication.</li>
            <li>Develops leadership, teamwork, and negotiation.</li>
            <li>Helps students understand international relations.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="info-card" style="margin-top:16px;">
        <h4>Basic MUN procedure</h4>
        <p class="procedure-text" style="text-align: justify;">
          In a typical committee, delegates present their country's stance through speeches.
          They then debate, ask questions, negotiate in moderated and unmoderated caucuses, and
          write draft resolutions. At the end, delegates vote on the resolutions. The goal is not
          only to enhance public speaking skills, but also to cooperate and create realistic solutions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="example-box">
        <h4>Position paper example</h4>
        <pre>Committee: Social, Humanitarian and Cultural Committee (SOCHUM)
Country: Arab Republic of Egypt
Agenda: Safeguarding Cultural Heritage in Conflict Zones

Safeguarding Cultural Heritage in Conflict Zones

In a period when radical militaristic actions are still being carried out, protecting a
nation's patrimonium may require significant effort, which can sometimes be unfeasible
if undertaken by a single state. Cultural heritage represents a pillar of a country's
history, serving as direct proof of the past and of the enigmas it has left to be deciphered.

The Arab Republic of Egypt is deeply aware of the importance of safeguarding its cultural
heritage despite the obstacles imposed by conflict and instability. Measures have been taken
to protect cultural heritage in several fields, including, but not limited to, the social and
legal spheres. Nevertheless, the challenges remain particularly difficult when cultural
genocide occurs. Cultural diplomacy is therefore of utmost importance in preventing unwanted
events, such as the looting that took place in Cairo in 2011.

Among the many examples of efforts to address this issue, Egypt has adopted various laws,
including Law No. 117 of 1983 and Law No. 144 of 2006, both of which support the preservation
of cultural heritage. Conflict zones make the situation even more challenging, especially when
terrorist organizations are also involved.

Thus, the Arab Republic of Egypt considers that one of the most effective ways to address the
obstacles threatening cultural heritage is to collaborate with INTERPOL, strengthen
international cooperation and promote the digitalization of endangered cultural elements.</pre>
      </div>

      <div class="example-box">
        <h4>Resolution example</h4>
